WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Speaker 1: We are going to hear from some&nbsp;
people in the audience you can call in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:12.080
via Discord David Pakman dot com slash discord,&nbsp;
and we're going to start today with Clark from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.760
Portland, Oregon. Clark, you're on the air.
Speaker 3: Hi, David. I was just wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.600
about what your
Speaker 4: stance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.760
on lowering the voting age. Obviously, you&nbsp;
know, teenagers pay taxes. Many teenagers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.480
can be emancipated from their parents. And I was&nbsp;
wondering what your stances on that? And also if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.120
you thought that ever be pushed through Congress.
Speaker 1: I think there is not a desire to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.080
people be able to vote at age 16 or 17, which&nbsp;
generally generally when people say lower the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.840
voting age, they mean 16 or 17. Is that what you&nbsp;
have in mind? Yeah, yeah. I think that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.960
a case to be made for that. I think it is widely&nbsp;
seen as being better for the left than the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.440
with the assumption that those 16 and 17 year&nbsp;
olds are more likely to vote for Democrats than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.200
Republicans. I don't know if that's actually&nbsp;
true. I think that that's something that I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:11.040
want to find data on. I am open to it. I think 17,&nbsp;
more than 16. In theory, the truth is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.720
the brain is not really fully formed at that point&nbsp;
in time. We know more and more about that. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.960
part of the reason why we're seeing some places&nbsp;
push smoking. You know, the age to buy cigarets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:31.040
looking at 21 instead of 18. I'm not necessarily&nbsp;
looking to to lower ages for things, certainly not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.640
for for driving. And in fact, as someone who got&nbsp;
my license when I was 16 and a half. And thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.920
back, my friends driving, it doesn't seem crazy to&nbsp;
raise the age to get your driver's license to 17&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.880
or 18. But I don't think that there's much of an&nbsp;
appetite among lawmakers to lower the voting age.&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.400
Speaker 4: Yes, certainly. It's just it's tough&nbsp;
to see, especially with all these crazy Trumpists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:58.160
that are probably a lot less educated than some&nbsp;
16 year olds in my circle. Yeah, and seeing like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.360
oh, like these guys probably can make a&nbsp;
much better and more informed her voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.280
decision than a lot of people out there.
Speaker 1: But there's no doubt that anecdotally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:12.240
there are lots of 17 year olds that would much be&nbsp;
much more educated as voters than many adults that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.000
vote regularly. That's without a doubt.
Speaker 4: Well, great, thanks, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.120
I'm glad. Glad to hear your opinion on that.
Speaker 1: Pleasure. Thank you so much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.280
the call. There is a clerk asking&nbsp;
about lowering the voting age.

